w:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: 【0†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org】 Report Issue 【1†Back to Abstract】 【2†Download PDF】 Contents Reading mode Colour theme\nL13:   1. 【3†Abstract】\nL14:   2. 【4†1 Introduction】\nL15:     1. 【5†1.1 Our Contributions】\nL16:     2. 【6†1.2 Paper Organization】\nL17:   3. 【7†2 Background and Motivation】\nL18:     1. 【8†2.1 Zero-Knowledge Proof Protocols】\nL19:   4. 【9†3 ZKPU Architecture】\nL20:     1. 【10†3.1 Twin Systolic Fabric】\nL21:     2. 【11†3.2 Deque buffer】\nL22:     3. 【12†3.3 ZK Engine】\nL23:     4. 【13†3.4 FIPS Montgomery Multiplication Illustration】\nL24:   5. 【14†4 Accelerating ZKP Protocols】\nL25:     1. 【15†4.1 Curve-based Protocols】\nL26:     2. 【16†4.2 Hash-based Protocols】\nL27:   6. 【17†5 Methodology & Evaluation】\nL28:     1. 【18†5.1 Methodology】\nL29:     2. 【19†5.2 Performance Evaluation.】\nL30:   7. 【20†6 Related Work】\nL31:   8. 【21†7 Conclusion】\nL32:   9. 【22†References】\nL33: 【23†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org】\nL34: \nL35: arXiv:2610.27530v1 [cs.AR] 20 Oct 2026\nL36: # ZKPU: Z ero-K nowledge P rocessing U nit for Heterogeneous Workloads\nL37: \nL38: Jonathan Ku^{1} Note: Correspondence E-mail: jonathan.ku@duke.edu    Changchun Zhou^{1}    Jiawen Wu^{2}    Sangouk Jeon^{3}    Cong Guo^{1}    Yuzhe Fu^{1}    Bowen Duan^{1}    Dongsuk Jeon^{3}    JV Rajendran^{2}    Yiran Chen^{1}    Hai “Helen” Li^{1}\nL39: ^{1}Duke University ^{2}Texas A&M University ^{3}Seoul National University\nL40: ###### Abstract\nL41: Zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) have become a fundamental building block for privacy-preserving computation, blockchain systems, and verifiable machine learning. Modern ZKP protocols span two distinct families: curve-based protocols, which rely on large finite-field arithmetic, and hash-based protocols, which operate over native 64-bit fields.\nL42: Existing hardware accelerators are specialized for one protocol family because they are built on operand-scanning Montgomery multiplication, resulting in fixed-width datapaths, poor utilization across heterogeneous operand widths, and limited scalability.\nL43: This paper presents ZKPU, a Zero-Knowledge Processing Unit for heterogeneous ZKP workloads. ZKPU introduces a new compute paradigm by co-designing finely integrated product scanning (FIPS) Montgomery multiplication with a reconfigurable Twin Systolic Fabric (TSF), enabling modular multiplication at arbitrary widths in 64-bit increments.\nL44: Combined with a fine-grained Deque buffer, on-chip twiddle-factor generator, and memory-efficient MSM preprocessing strategy, ZKPU provides a unified architecture for both curve-based and hash-based protocols.\nL45: Evaluation using Groth16 and Plonky2 shows that ZKPU achieves up to $144\\times$ speedup for NTT, $13.6\\times$ for MSM, and approximately $7.2\\times$ end-to-end application speedup over state-of-the-art accelerators, while being the first architecture to efficiently support both protocol families on a single reconfigurable hardware platform.\nL173: ## 5 Methodology & Evaluation\nL174: Table 3: Area and power breakdown of previous works.\nL175: Work  | Technology Node  | Area ($mm^{2}$)  | Power (W)\nL176: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL177: ZKPU  | 16nm  | 24.93  | 11.95\nL178: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL179: PipeZK  | 28nm  | 52.91  | 7.04\nL180: LegoZK  | 28nm  | 68.79  | 11.39\nL181: UniZK  | 7nm  | 57.8  | 96.4\nL182: Figure 9: Core kernels in curve-based ZKP protocols: NTT and MSM kernel latency of ZKPU compared with previous works under 300MHz. (a,b) NTT latency vs. transform size at 256-, and 768-bit operand widths. (c,d) MSM latency vs. number of points at 256-, and 768-bit operand widths.\nL183: ### 5.1 Methodology\nL184: Architecture Implementation. To evaluate ZKPU, we develop a cycle-accurate simulator that models the core computational kernels of both curve-based and hash-based ZKP protocols. The simulator maps modular arithmetic operations onto the proposed architecture and evaluates Groth16 and Plonky2 as representative protocols. The key hardware components of ZKPU are implemented in Verilog and synthesized using Synopsys Design Compiler with the TSMC 16nm technology PDK at 300MHz to obtain area and power estimates.\nL185: For comparison, we reproduce representative ZKP accelerators, including PipeZK and LegoZK for curve-based protocols and UniZK for hash-based protocols. Since these designs were originally reported under different technology nodes and implementation constraints, we normalize the results using DeepScaleTool [【69†32】] and compare all architectures under unified scaling methodology. We further report the scaled latency to jointly evaluate the impact of technology node and area.\nL186: Figure 10: Area and power breakdown of ZKPU in 16nm. The PEs within TSFs dominate in area while both TSF and Deque buffer have similar contribution to power.\nL266:   * [31] N. Samardzic, S. Langowski, S. Devadas, and D. Sanchez (2024) Accelerating zero-knowledge proofs through hardware-algorithm co-design. In 2024 57th IEEE/ACM International Symposium on Microarchitecture (MICRO), pp. 366–379. Cited by: 【88†§6】.\nL267:   * [32] S. Sarangi and B. Baas (2021) Deepscaletool: a tool for the accurate estimation of technology scaling in the deep-submicron era. In 2021 IEEE International Symposium on Circuits and Systems (ISCAS), pp. 1–5. Cited by: 【100†§5.1】.\nL268:   * [33] P. Z. Team (2022) Plonky2: fast recursive arguments with plonk and fri. Technical report Polygon Labs. External Links: 【101†Link†github.com】 Cited by: 【90†§1】.\nL269:   * [34] C. Wang and M. Gao (2023) Sam: a scalable accelerator for number theoretic transform using multi-dimensional decomposition. In 2023 IEEE/ACM International Conference on Computer Aided Design (ICCAD), pp. 1–9. Cited by: 【88†§6】.\nL270:   * [35] C. Wang and M. Gao (2025) Unizk: accelerating zero-knowledge proof with unified hardware and flexible kernel mapping. In Proceedings of the 30th ACM International Conference on Architectural Support for Programming Languages and Operating Systems, Volume 1, pp. 1101–1117. Cited by: 【102†§4.2】, 【103†§6】.\nL271:   * [36] Charles. F. Xavier (2022) PipeMSM: hardware acceleration for multi-scalar multiplication. Note: Cryptology ePrint Archive, Paper 2022/999 External Links: 【104†Link†eprint.iacr.org】 Cited by: 【88†§6】.\nL272:   * [37] Y. Xie, Y. Liu, X. Zheng, B. Lan, D. Lei, D. Xiang, S. Cai, and X. Xiong (2024) FLALM: a flexible low area-latency montgomery modular multiplication on fpga. IEEE Transactions on Computers 74 (1), pp. 29–42. Cited by: 【96†§2.1】.\nL273:   * [38] Z. Yang, L. Zhao, P. Li, H. Liu, K. Li, B. Zhao, D. Meng, and R. Hou (2025) Legozk: a dynamically reconfigurable accelerator for zero-knowledge proof. In 2025 IEEE International Symposium on High Performance Computer Architecture (HPCA), pp. 113–126. Cited by: 【105†§1】, 【106†§3.1】, 【107†§4.1】, 【108†§4.1】, 【97†§4.1】, 【99†§4.1】, 【109†§5.1】, 【88†§6】.\nL274:   * [39] Starky: fast stark proving system External Links: 【110†Link†github.com】 Cited by: 【90†§1】.\nL275:   * [40] J. Zhang, Z. Fang, Y. Zhang, and D. Song (2020) Zero knowledge proofs for decision tree predictions and accuracy. In Proceedings of the 2020 ACM SIGSAC Conference on Computer and Communications Security, pp. 2039–2053. Cited by: 【90†§1】, 【89†§2.1】.\nL276:   * [41] J. Zhang, M. Imani, and E. Sadredini (2023) Bp-ntt: fast and compact in-sram number theoretic transform with bit-parallel modular multiplication. In 2023 60th ACM/IEEE Design Automation Conference (DAC), pp. 1–6. Cited by: 【88†§6】.\nL277:   * [42] Y. Zhang, S. Wang, X. Zhang, J. Dong, X. Mao, F. Long, C. Wang, D. Zhou, M. Gao, and G. Sun (2021) Pipezk: accelerating zero-knowledge proof with a pipelined architecture. In 2021 ACM/IEEE 48th Annual International Symposium on Computer Architecture (ISCA), pp. 416–428. Cited by: 【111†§2.1】, 【107†§4.1】, 【109†§5.1】, 【88†§6】.\nL278:   * [43] B. Zhao, W. Huang, T. Li, and Y. Huang (2023) Bstmsm: a high-performance fpga-based multi-scalar multiplication hardware accelerator. In 2023 International Conference on Field Programmable Technology (ICFPT), pp. 35–43. Cited by: 【88†§6】.\nL279:   * [44] X. Zhu, H. He, Z. Yang, Y. Deng, L. Zhao, and R. Hou (2024) Elastic msm: a fast, elastic and modular preprocessing technique for multi-scalar multiplication algorithm on gpus. IACR Transactions on Cryptographic Hardware and Embedded Systems 2024 (4), pp. 258–284. Cited by: 【88†§6】.\nL280: Experimental support, please 【112†view the build logs】 for errors. Generated by 【113†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov】 .\nL281: ## Instructions for reporting errors\nL282: \nL283: We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below:\nL284: \nL285:   * Click the \"Report Issue\" () button, located in the page header.\nL286: \nL287: Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - THU-HAS/MegaZK · GitHub (https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK)\nciteturn5view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view0\",\"pattern\":\"KNOWN_ISSUE\"}); Total lines: 383\nL232:   * 【110†`plonky2_ed25519`】: experimental Ed25519 benchmark. Proof generation is expected to run, but verification currently has a known issue (`BENCHMARK_VERIFY_STATUS: KNOWN_ISSUE`). That is not a regression.\nL233:   * 【111†`plonky2-gpu-bench`】: std-only internal helper for shared benchmark timing, verification policy, and CUDA memory reporting.\nL234: ## Current Reproduction Environment\nL235: \nL236: The following environment is the current known reproduction baseline. Other versions may work, but have not been normalized for this open-source snapshot.\nL237:   * OS: Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS, Linux 6.8.0-124-generic, x86_64\nL238:   * Rust: `nightly-2023-11-28` (pinned in 【99†`rust-toolchain.toml`】)\nL239:     * `rustc 1.76.0-nightly (49b3924bd 2023-11-27)`\nL240:     * `cargo 1.76.0-nightly (9b13310ca 2023-11-24)`\nL241:   * CUDA toolkit: 12.x (this snapshot was built with 12.5, `nvcc V12.5.82`)\nL242:   * CMake: 3.30.3\nL243:   * clang: 14.0.0\nL244:   * GPU/driver: requires an NVIDIA GPU and driver compatible with CUDA 12.x. If CMake cannot detect the GPU architecture during build, set `CUDAARCHS` manually, for example `CUDAARCHS=89`.\nL245: The repository has a pinned toolchain in 【99†`rust-toolchain.toml`】. For CPU baseline comparisons, `RUSTFLAGS=-Ctarget-cpu=native` can improve performance on the local machine.\nL246: ## Support Matrix\nL247: \nL248: Benchmark  | GPU proof  | CPU baseline  | Verification status\nL249: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL250: Factorial  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL251: Fibonacci  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL252: SHA256  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL253: SHA512  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL254: ECDSA  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL255: MVM  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL256: Ed25519  | Experimental  | Experimental  | Known verification issue\nL257: Ed25519: prove is expected to run; verify is a known failure (`KNOWN_ISSUE`), not a regression.\nL258: ## Benchmark Circuit Sources\nL259: \nL260: These are the open-source circuit / gadget implementations used by each benchmark (not the proving-system libraries). Commands below are this repository's CLI; full smoke and paper-scale invocations are in 【112†Build And Run】. Paper numbers in brackets are ICS '26 camera-ready references. No pinned commit is recorded in this snapshot, so links go to the upstream repository root.\nL261:   * Factorial — run: `-p plonky2 --example factorial` (for example `-- --num 2000`). Circuit: 【113†0xPolygonZero/plonky2】 (paper [27]; in-tree 【114†`plonky2/plonky2/examples/factorial.rs`】).\nL262:   * Fibonacci — run: `-p plonky2 --example fibonacci` (for example `-- --num 2000`). Circuit: 【113†0xPolygonZero/plonky2】 (paper [27]; in-tree 【115†`plonky2/plonky2/examples/fibonacci.rs`】).\nL263:   * SHA256 — run: `-p plonky2_sha256` (for example `-- --num 1`). Circuit: 【116†polymerdao/plonky2-sha256】 (paper [30]; in-tree 【106†`plonky2-sha256/`】).\nL264:   * SHA512 — run: `-p plonky2_sha512` (for example `-- --num 1`). Circuit: 【117†polymerdao/plonky2-sha512】 (paper [31]; in-tree 【107†`plonky2-sha512/`】).\nL265:   * ECDSA — run: `-p plonky2-examples --example 4_ecdsa_verification` (for example `-- --num 1`). Circuit example: 【118†qope/plonky2-examples】 (paper [36]; in-tree 【108†`plonky2-examples/`】). ECDSA gadget used by that example: 【119†0xPolygonZero/plonky2-ecdsa】 (in-tree 【120†`plonky2-ecdsa/`】).\nL266:   * Ed25519 — run: `-p plonky2_ed25519` (for example `-- --num 1`). Circuit vendored in this tree: 【121†Electron-Labs/plonky2_ed25519】 (in-tree 【110†`plonky2_ed25519/`】). Paper [29] cites 【122†polymerdao/plonky2-ed25519】; the files here match Electron-Labs (authors, `curve25519-dalek` git rev, and layout), not the polymerdao tree.\nL267:   * MVM — run: `-p neural-zkp --bin rust-app` (for example `-- --input-size 100 --output-size 100`). Circuit: 【123†worldcoin/proto-neural-zkp】 (paper [43]; in-tree 【109†`proto-neural-zkp/`】). The vendored `Cargo.toml` still lists `https://github.com/dcbuild3r/proto-neural-zkp/` as `repository`; that is a contributor copy of the Worldcoin project.\nL268: ## Build And Run\nL269: \nL270: Run the following commands from the repository root.\nL271: \nL272: The default feature set is CPU-only. Use `--features gpu` for the GPU prover, and `--baseline` for the CPU prover in the benchmark CLIs.\nL273: \nL274: This is a root Cargo workspace. Commands below use `--locked` so they build from the checked-in root `Cargo.lock`. rustup will pick `nightly-2023-11-28` from 【99†`rust-toolchain.toml`】. GPU builds need CUDA 12.x, an NVIDIA driver, and (when CMake cannot detect the architecture) `CUDAARCHS`.\nL275: The commands in this README are this repository's CLI.\nL276: Every benchmark reports `Time taken to build:`, `Time taken to prove:`, and `Time taken to verify:` using Rust `Duration` formatting. GPU runs also report used and free CUDA memory with a shared format. MVM retains its existing seven-column CSV output in addition to these human-readable timings. A completed verification also emits exactly one `BENCHMARK_VERIFY_STATUS: PASS`, `BENCHMARK_VERIFY_STATUS: KNOWN_ISSUE`, or `BENCHMARK_VERIFY_STATUS: FAIL` machine-readable status line.\nL277: ### Smoke (small workloads)\nL278: \nL279: #### Factorial\nL280: \nL281:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --features gpu --example factorial -- --num 2000\nL282:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --example factorial -- --num 2000 --baseline\nL283: \nL284: #### Fibonacci\nL285: \nL286:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --features gpu --example fibonacci -- --num 2000\nL287:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --example fibonacci -- --num 2000 --baseline\nL288: #### SHA256\nL289: \nL290:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha256 --features gpu -- --num 1\nL291:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha256 -- --num 1 --baseline\nL292: \nL293: #### SHA512\nL294: \nL295:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha512 --features gpu -- --num 1\nL296:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha512 -- --num 1 --baseline\nL297: #### ECDSA\nL298: \nL299:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2-examples --features gpu --example 4_ecdsa_verification -- --num 1\nL300:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2-examples --example 4_ecdsa_verification -- --num 1 --baseline\nL301: #### Ed25519 Experimental\nL302: \nL303:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_ed25519 --features gpu -- --num 1\nL304:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_ed25519 -- --num 1 --baseline\nL305: \nL306: Ed25519 proof generation is expected to run. A verification failure after proof generation is a known issue (`BENCHMARK_VERIFY_STATUS: KNOWN_ISSUE`) and should not be treated as a regression.\nL307: #### MVM\nL308: \nL309:     cargo run --locked --release -p neural-zkp --features gpu --bin rust-app -- --input-size 100 --output-size 100\nL310:     cargo run --locked --release -p neural-zkp --bin rust-app -- --input-size 100 --output-size 100 --baseline\nL311: ### Paper-scale examples\nL312: \nL313: `--memory-profile` defaults to `compressed`. Paper-scale runs need large GPU memory (about 24GB class). Factorial and Fibonacci paper-scale sorting is very slow; prove can still run.\nL314: Uncompressed (paper-U size flags):\nL315: \nL316:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --features gpu --example factorial -- --num 131073 --memory-profile uncompressed\nL317:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --features gpu --example fibonacci -- --num 262143 --memory-profile uncompressed\nL318:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha256 --features gpu -- --num 60 --memory-profile uncompressed\nL319:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha512 --features gpu -- --num 20 --memory-profile uncompressed\nL320:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2-examples --features gpu --example 4_ecdsa_verification -- --num 6 --seed 5eed5eed5eed5eed --memory-profile uncompressed\nL321:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_ed25519 --features gpu -- --num 2 --memory-profile uncompressed\nL322:     cargo run --locked --release -p neural-zkp --features gpu --bin rust-app -- --input-size 4000 --output-size 4000 --coefficient-bits 16 --num-wires 400 --num-routed-wires 400 --seed 5eed5eed5eed5eed --memory-profile uncompressed\nL323: Compressed (paper-C size flags; default profile is already `compressed`):\nL324: \nL325:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --features gpu --example factorial -- --num 524289 --memory-profile compressed\nL326:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2 --features gpu --example fibonacci -- --num 1048575 --memory-profile compressed\nL327:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha256 --features gpu -- --num 210 --memory-profile compressed\nL328:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_sha512 --features gpu -- --num 137 --memory-profile compressed\nL329:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2-examples --features gpu --example 4_ecdsa_verification -- --num 42 --seed 5eed5eed5eed5eed --memory-profile compressed\nL330:     cargo run --locked --release -p plonky2_ed25519 --features gpu -- --num 11 --memory-profile compressed\nL331:     cargo run --locked --release -p neural-zkp --features gpu --bin rust-app -- --input-size 9000 --output-size 9000 --coefficient-bits 16 --num-wires 400 --num-routed-wires 400 --seed 5eed5eed5eed5eed --memory-profile compressed\nL332: Ed25519 paper-scale: prove is expected to run; verify remains the same known failure (`KNOWN_ISSUE`).\nL333: ## Notes For Contributors\nL334:   * This repository is a root Cargo workspace. Use `--locked` for reproducible builds against the checked-in root `Cargo.lock`.\nL335:   * The `plonky2` crate and benchmark packages keep CUDA support behind their non-default `gpu` feature. Targeted default builds are CPU-only.\nL336:   * The GPU path uses `CircuitBuilder::build_gpu` with witness input targets, followed by `GpuCircuitData::prove` with an equally sized witness input slice. GPU proofs continue to use the CPU verifier through `GpuCircuitData::verify`.\nL337:   * Verbose proving features may produce JSON debug dumps. These files are local artifacts and should not be committed.\nL338: ## Acknowledgments\nL339: \nL340: Proving-system libraries vendored in this tree (not the benchmark circuits above). Details are in 【97†`NOTICE`】:\nL341: \nL342:   * plonky2: 【113†0xPolygonZero/plonky2】 (also 【124†mir-protocol/plonky2】); this copy lives in 【103†`plonky2/`】\nL343:   * era-boojum: 【125†matter-labs/era-boojum】 (【126†`era-boojum/`】)\nL344:   * era-cuda: 【127†matter-labs/era-cuda】 (【105†`era-cuda/`】)\nL345:   * era-boojum-cuda: 【128†matter-labs/era-boojum-cuda】 (【104†`era-boojum-cuda/`】)\nL346: ## About\nL347: \nL348: No description, website, or topics provided.\nL349: \nL350: ### Resources\nL351: \nL352: 【71†Readme】\nL353: \nL354: License and 2 other licenses found\nL355: \nL356: 【72†Activity】\nL357: \nL358: 【73†Custom properties】\nL359: \nL360: ### Stars\nL361: \nL362: 1 star\nL363: \nL364: ### Watchers\nL365: \nL366: 0 watching\nL367: \nL368: ### Forks\nL369: \nL370: 【74†0 forks】\nL371: \nL372: 【75†Report repository】\nL373: \nL374: ## Releases\nL375: \nL376: ## Packages\nL377: \nL378: ## Contributors\nL379: \nL380: ## Languages\nL381: \nL382: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n"
